#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087489 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7703063b-658d-50a5-8ac3-747b7f029bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at the Origin"}] :question/id #uuid "b798cfbb-89ac-5d31-adb5-5c43bd483226" :question/math-academy-id "e-5374" :question/problem "Consider the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown below. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(2,0).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/156d160267551e9dbf4db42b14b1e0dbb46ab658a836a760cf31a42eb89662d3.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$ centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius is $r = 2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087490 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0346056c-cf8e-51bf-92f3-052a0948a6fa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at a General Point"}] :question/id #uuid "140fc720-22c0-5832-afae-c255144291d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5375" :question/problem "Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ that generates a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(4,6).$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx= r\\cos\\theta+a,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta+b,\\quad  \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(4,6).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta+4,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta+6,\\quad\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087551 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d81c7ff-8459-5559-bfae-c2a9c3bc739e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "4ffaeeac-95a0-598a-9880-166d03f810ce" :question/math-academy-id "e-6779" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/f44fad12ad1f7d465423f92258caae38e0c536153ba2c3c3a6de573061c69993.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx = 6\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 6\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r = 6.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/4bd384e2b1a9d0568617ddbfd5ac7dbe9630882b45c40d7912f4809a6e0be551.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $\\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right],$ we get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in \\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087555 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parametrized Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "8bff424d-8f2e-528b-acf0-12c33958a132" :question/math-academy-id "e-6817" :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta + 2,\\quad y=3\\sin\\theta - 4, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta\\lt 2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{3} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+4}{3} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $(x-2)^2 + (y+4)^2 = 9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7703063b-658d-50a5-8ac3-747b7f029bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9c61364-89e4-5ec3-a514-1fa886fddcb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=225\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=225\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285397} {:answer/id #uuid "3ca2e245-1032-5752-ade8-a7803af1ea17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{15}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{15}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285398} {:answer/id #uuid "bf6b8347-427c-5ff8-8fe1-dfdc0044329b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=30\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=30\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285399} {:answer/id #uuid "f73e5d31-eedb-523d-9bb1-d5f27f8eea2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=15\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=15\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285400} {:answer/id #uuid "e29315a3-c81d-5a6c-9918-c51edc8afbcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\sqrt{15}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f73e5d31-eedb-523d-9bb1-d5f27f8eea2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=15\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=15\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285400} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c35d5ed-8908-579f-bd21-599749407541" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "384a4738-eb41-5445-8951-5cd80374c2fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-105915" :question/problem "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $15$ centered at the origin?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=15.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=15\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=15\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285402 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7703063b-658d-50a5-8ac3-747b7f029bb3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f658a318-42b0-511b-b456-f8782fa2ff34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285404} {:answer/id #uuid "15dbfcfe-475a-5c2e-918f-6a6f36b3b368" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285405} {:answer/id #uuid "d3d27925-46e3-5fab-81ed-7dd3554301bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285406} {:answer/id #uuid "45f9084b-8eba-58fb-9c77-f376633271d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285407} {:answer/id #uuid "7a4462af-e83e-5b61-be83-021ec8c564ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285408}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3d27925-46e3-5fab-81ed-7dd3554301bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285406} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee7cc81e-a46d-5c5c-97bd-25350773fc2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "821d7677-e14f-54cf-a344-d6f2cb89596c" :question/math-academy-id "q-106032" :question/problem "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at the origin?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0346056c-cf8e-51bf-92f3-052a0948a6fa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a93ff279-b10b-53ce-97a8-65c746433fa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285439} {:answer/id #uuid "d4bf81b9-2223-58e7-b783-70cabb89f670" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285440} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb33231-df72-5ca8-8640-950b7f6b722b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285441} {:answer/id #uuid "f2e078bf-e8b9-5d98-a4bb-cdc4277ee5f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285442} {:answer/id #uuid "4920736c-ec67-5e91-ac7d-3d520193c11d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bb33231-df72-5ca8-8640-950b7f6b722b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285441} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b463a27-3a4a-5b59-93d3-df9bc4c770d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3b4cc6ec-a5c3-5145-a122-c3b8c6352b9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-106693" :question/problem "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(5,-3)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(5,-3).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285470 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d81c7ff-8459-5559-bfae-c2a9c3bc739e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd8ff7a2-0f61-525d-a304-ad1c14eb6d8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285472} {:answer/id #uuid "9601c170-7b2e-5c66-9342-9a6a3b9dfb3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9601c170-7b2e-5c66-9342-9a6a3b9dfb3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285473} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2fedb1f-671d-57c8-aadc-46276312262d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285471} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "601ca41a-42dc-5d5a-9575-3ff7aa048d06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "601ca41a-42dc-5d5a-9575-3ff7aa048d06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285475} :answer-field/id #uuid "f063c384-0a90-5a58-b263-bace855ed6ae" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f6fa581-0ad2-5257-8314-2e5550da3def" :question/math-academy-id "q-321948" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/ed6f528f7e2b873812d492cbeec386ab56af626a964e98b2929824ec9075d320.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=4.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/682f73ccec945de2789ecca71aa6dae650c485189c60ffc64ab7a7aa108665da.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0346056c-cf8e-51bf-92f3-052a0948a6fa" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40208fa8-350f-5d1a-bfb6-eb79193040b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285506} {:answer/id #uuid "dae8a998-a3e3-5155-9d91-acd1f198e8fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285507} {:answer/id #uuid "67666ee2-7893-5d32-9acd-bee7f015aae0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285508} {:answer/id #uuid "a6bda015-db44-5c8f-918e-363ef8e23925" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285509} {:answer/id #uuid "0e1ef3a4-aaa8-5bf5-b55f-bc2cf177e401" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e1ef3a4-aaa8-5bf5-b55f-bc2cf177e401" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285510} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b07eb1b-4626-5bc8-ab3d-16bab8d6e553" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e74c508-de38-5c1f-966f-ae4879cf576c" :question/math-academy-id "q-79192" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/6b77b1b8a61b5ec7306fbc0b3d70618e7fdce2237bdcbfd017c23c01b80f2979.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $3$ centered at $(-4,5)$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(-1,5)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r+a,b).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(-4,5).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285518 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d334fa1-725c-5687-9ed2-83a5788db925" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parametrized Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fb737d9-6de8-5e97-8000-4ef442ee10f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285520} {:answer/id #uuid "26a22685-aa53-52bf-9042-affb9261b909" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285521} {:answer/id #uuid "8cb053a0-af9a-58ad-953f-75f723563c8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285522} {:answer/id #uuid "f2a500cb-13e8-5c35-94fb-220d40d5cd03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285523} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b156e2-d677-5816-8578-ade01ccf161a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2}=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1fb737d9-6de8-5e97-8000-4ef442ee10f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285520} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7f557df-3792-5d35-9fc6-05c142f12f3c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285519}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "480062d9-9533-5d48-bc7f-73b46822f0d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-79221" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+2}{3} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+3}{3} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{9} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $(x+2)^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=9.$"}]}
